course: dictionary
lesson: 8468
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The clues around the word show you which meaning fits. Salon is a good example: where you find it tells you what it means.
explain: «I need a haircut, so I'm going to the hair salon on Main Street.» The clues are haircut and hair, so a salon here is a shop where hair is cut or styled. Beauty salon and nail salon work the same way: they are shops for looking after your skin or nails.
explain: In a history lesson, salon means something very different. «In the 1700s, writers and thinkers met at a rich person's salon in France to talk about books and ideas.» The clues are writers, thinkers and books, so a salon here is a gathering, not a shop.
explain: French words can help too. «Voici la cuisine, à côté il y a le salon, et ma chambre est au premier étage» means: here is the kitchen, next to it the living room, and my bedroom is one floor up. So le salon is the living room.
fact: A salon is a shop where people have their hair cut or styled, their nails done or their skin treated.
fact: In history, a salon was a meeting of writers, artists and thinkers at a rich person's home, especially in France.
fact: A salon can be an art exhibition, such as the famous Paris Salon.
fact: In French, «le salon» means the living room, and «la salle à manger» means the dining room.
exercise: «I'm going to the hair salon to get my hair cut.» Where is the person going? | A bank for money | *A shop where hair is cut or styled | A big kitchen for cooking | A school for art
exercise: «The writers and thinkers met at the salon to talk about their ideas.» What is a salon here? | A football pitch | *A meeting of clever people for conversation | A bedroom | A railway station
exercise: «Voici la cuisine, à côté il y a le salon, et ma chambre est au premier étage.» What does salon mean in this French sentence? | A hairdresser's | *A living room | A bedroom upstairs | A kitchen
test: A nail salon is a place where you go to... | sell old books | learn to play the piano | buy fresh fish | *have your nails done
test: «Writers and artists met at a rich person's salon in the 1700s.» What kind of salon is this? | A room for cooking meals | An art exhibition in a gallery | *A gathering of writers and thinkers | A shop for hair and nails
exam: Which phrase means a place where your nails are done? | nail biting | nail file | nail varnish remover | *nail salon
